{#
The docked pane's body, inside its own Turbo Frame.

Navigating within the pane — a different week, a different view — replaces only
this, so the mail beside it never reloads and never loses its scroll position.
Every link inside targets the frame by name; without that, clicking "next week"
in the pane would navigate the whole page to a calendar and take the mail away.
#}

{% set frame = 'calendar-pane-frame' %}

<turbo-frame id="{{ frame }}" class="flex flex-col min-h-0 flex-1">
    {# `data-pane-min-width` is read by ui--split on turbo:frame-load: choosing a
       view that needs seven columns widens the pane to fit it rather than
       drawing seven slivers. Rendered per view, because the view is what has
       the requirement — see CalendarView::minimumPaneWidth().

       INSIDE the frame rather than on it, and that is the whole reason this
       wrapper exists. Turbo replaces a frame's CONTENTS and leaves the frame
       element's own attributes as they were, so a value written on the
       <turbo-frame> tag is read once on first load and never updated again —
       the pane would widen for whichever view it happened to open on and then
       stop reacting. `display: contents` so the wrapper changes no layout: its
       children are laid out by the frame exactly as if it were not here. #}
    <div class="contents" data-pane-min-width="{{ view.minimumPaneWidth }}">
        {% include 'calendar/_toolbar.html.twig' with { compact: true } %}
        {# `frame` is set above rather than passed here, because the grid does
           not use it itself — _view_month's "N more" link does, two includes
           down, and an `include ... with` would have to be repeated at every
           level to get it there. #}
        {% include 'calendar/_grid.html.twig' %}
    </div>
</turbo-frame>
